      A coffered ceiling commands attention because of its mathematical harmony. When beam widths, vertical drop depths, and coffer box proportions follow classical architectural canons, the room feels grand, balanced, and serene. If proportions are chosen arbitrarily, beams look like heavy wooden gutters crushing the room or flimsy applied moldings. Here are the architectural formulas to master the <strong>1:12 depth-to-height rule</strong> and calculate <strong>symmetrical coffer box grids</strong>.
