    <h2>1. The 1:12 Architectural Beam Depth Canon</h2>
    <p>
      In classical Renaissance and Beaux-Arts architecture, beam drop depth is directly indexed to the overall vertical scale of the space. The universal baseline for residential box-beam framing is the <strong>1:12 proportion</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      Beam Depth Formula:<br/>
      Maximum Recommended Depth = Ceiling Height (in feet) x 1.0 Inch<br/>
      • 8-Foot Ceiling: 4.0" to 5.5" Shallow Drop (or 1.5" faux batten beam)<br/>
      • 9-Foot Ceiling: 6.0" to 7.5" Moderate Drop<br/>
      • 10-Foot Ceiling: 8.0" to 10.0" Full Classical Drop<br/>
      • 12-Foot Ceiling: 10.0" to 14.0" Grand Cathedral Drop
    </div>
    <p>
      Dropping deeper than 1:10 (e.g., 12-inch beams on a 9-foot ceiling) causes the ceiling plane to feel oppressive, casting heavy shadow lines and visually shrinking room perceived volume. Conversely, building beams shallower than 1:20 (e.g., 3-inch drops on an 11-foot ceiling) results in a weak, cardboard-thin look that fails to create the deep shadow relief essential to a genuine coffer.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Architectural Proportion Sizing Table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
