    <h2>3. Mathematical Grid Symmetry: Rectangular Room Solutions</h2>
    <p>
      Residential rooms are almost never perfectly square. In a 15-ft x 20-ft great room, attempting to force perfectly square coffer boxes (e.g., 4 ft x 4 ft) leaves awkward, mismatched leftover border strips along the perimeter walls (e.g., 3-inch strips along one wall and 18-inch strips along the other).
    </p>
    <p>
      To achieve harmonious balance, follow the <strong>equal division rule</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Select the number of coffer bays along the length and width (e.g., 3 bays wide by 4 bays long, creating a 12-box grid).</li>
      <li>Calculate the aspect ratio: in a 15x20 room with a 3x4 grid:
        <ul>
          <li>Width bay size: 15 ft / 3 bays = 5.0 ft</li>
          <li>Length bay size: 20 ft / 4 bays = 5.0 ft</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Notice the result: by dividing the 15-ft width into 3 sections and the 20-ft length into 4 sections, <strong>every single coffer box is a flawless 5-foot by 5-foot square!</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Even when numbers do not divide evenly into perfect squares, the human eye cannot detect an aspect ratio deviation between 1.0 and 1.20 (e.g., a 44-inch by 48-inch box appears perfectly square from the floor looking up).
    </p>

    <h2>4. Net Sunken Coffer Calculation (Accounting for Beam Width)</h2>
    <p>
      When measuring for recessed drywall panels, acoustic wood planks, or wallpaper inserts inside each sunken coffer, always subtract the beam width from the on-center grid spacing:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-100 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-800 rounded-lg my-4">
      Net Opening Formula:<br/>
      Net Coffer Width = (On-Center Grid Spacing) - (Full Beam Width)<br/>
      Example: If grid centers are 48 inches and beams are 6 inches wide:<br/>
      Net Drywall Opening = 48" - 6" = 42" Clear Sunken Square
    </div>

    <h2>5. Structural Joist Alignment Strategy</h2>
    <p>
      A ceiling consists of 2x8, 2x10, or engineered I-joists running in one direction at 16" or 24" on-center. To ensure structural integrity:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Primary Running Beams (Continuous):</strong> Orient your primary continuous 2x4 framing ladders <strong>perpendicular to the ceiling joists</strong>. This guarantees that your main running beam crosses and securely anchors into a solid ceiling joist every 16 or 24 inches.</li>
      <li><strong>The Secondary Cross Beams (Cross-Tees):</strong> The perpendicular cross-tees run parallel to joists. If a cross-tee aligns with a joist bay, you must install 2x4 bridging blocks between the joists inside the attic floor or ceiling plenum to provide structural screw bite every 24 inches.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

