    note="A critical design flaw that ruins an otherwise immaculate coffered ceiling is failing to align the ceiling grid with the room's major architectural features. When you stand in the entrance of a room, your eye immediately seeks symmetry between the fireplace mantel, the center dining table chandelier, the kitchen island pendant grouping, and the coffered ceiling beams. If a box beam passes 4 inches off-center across a stately masonry fireplace opening, or forces a dining chandelier to hang awkwardly 3 inches away from a beam intersection, the entire room feels distorted. Always begin your ceiling layout from the room's primary focal centerline (such as the center of the fireplace or the main entry sightline) and project your grid outward toward the perimeter walls."
  />

